  "slug": "expanding-glycomics-0644",
  "summary": "Growing sample scales and single-cell datasets demand storage and analysis software that stays fast, compact and reproducible.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is making genomic computation scale and remain portable across cohorts, modalities and languages. pbzarr proposes a Zarr-based format for per-base data across thousands of samples, reported as faster and smaller than D4 and bigWig. RapidGLM addresses the compute side, a C++ library with multiple interfaces that speeds genome-scale count-regression analyses for differential expression, CRISPR screens and eQTL mapping by 2.5-17.8x. CyteArc applies out-of-core processing to single-cell RNA, ATAC, protein and multi-omic data, scaling to over 100M cells. The Fusion transpilation study addresses reproducibility rather than scale, showing identical outputs across nine language implementations of the St. Nicolas House Analysis algorithm. What is new is the joint emphasis on storage formats, scalable statistical kernels and verifiable cross-language implementations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
